ProsperoSunshine: every per-device decision uses the certificate of the request's own connection

Upstream kept the client certificate of the last TLS handshake in a process-wide value and read it when a request arrived. A
client may send its request up to timeout_request (5 s) after its handshake, and any other client's handshake in between
replaced the value, so a request could be judged with another paired device's certificate. The PS5 host builds its limits on
that certificate: hidden apps and "PS5 Screen" (patch 0023), the profiles a device's controllers play as (0016), the keyboard
and mouse owner (0022), quitting only the own stream (0012), and the stream list. A limited device could time its request to
follow a trusted device's handshake (Moonlight polls /serverinfo every few seconds) and act as that device.

The global is gone. Each HTTPS handler that needs the client (applist, launch, resume, cancel, appasset) takes the certificate
from its own connection's TLS session (Request::socket(), Simple-Web-Server patch 0001), checks it again against the paired
devices, as it may have been unpaired or disabled since the handshake, and refuses with 401 when there is none. Requests on
the same connection read the same TLS session, so neither a reused address and port nor keep-alive can mix clients up.

Two checks that need the right certificate come with it: /cancel closes the game (close_game_on_quit) only for the device whose
launch started it or a device trusted with every app (process_ps5.cpp), and /appasset gives the default cover for an app the
device may not see, so it can't tell that a hidden title is installed.

